--- !u!114 &511381462
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 511381458}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2d04508e9b9c04dbc8687050da461868, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MissionContentManager
  missions:
  - missionId: solar_shield
    missionTitle: Solar Shield Battery Check
    crewQuery: Can we activate the solar shield during low battery mode?
    evidenceCards:
    - evidenceId: shield_power_requirement
      evidenceText: The solar shield requires the battery level to be above 40%.
      idealRank: 1
    - evidenceId: low_battery_threshold
      evidenceText: Low battery mode begins when the battery level falls below 25%.
      idealRank: 2
    - evidenceId: oxygen_backup_power
      evidenceText: The oxygen system uses backup power during low battery mode.
      idealRank: 0
    - evidenceId: radiation_protection
      evidenceText: The solar shield protects the ship during radiation storms.
      idealRank: 0
    groundedAnswers:
    - No. Low battery mode means the battery is below 25%, while the solar shield
      requires above 40%. The shield should remain offline. Supported by {KEY1} and
      {KEY2}.
    - The solar shield should not be activated. It requires more than 40% battery,
      but low battery mode begins below 25%. Supported by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    partialAnswers:
    - Activation may be unsafe because the available evidence indicates a battery
      restriction, but the full threshold comparison is missing.
    - The ranked evidence provides only partial support, so the crew should not make
      the shield decision yet.
    riskyAnswers:
    - Yes. The solar shield can be activated because low battery mode uses backup
      power.
    - The shield should work during low battery mode because radiation protection
      is always required.
    successConsequence: The shield remains offline. Battery power is preserved for
      essential systems.
    delayedConsequence: The crew pauses the decision while battery reserves continue
      to fall.
    failureConsequence: The shield overloads the low-battery system and causes a
      shipwide power emergency.
  - missionId: cargo_bay
    missionTitle: Cargo Bay Safety Check
    crewQuery: Can we open the exterior cargo bay while the ship is moving at cruise
      speed?
    evidenceCards:
    - evidenceId: cargo_speed_requirement
      evidenceText: Exterior cargo bay doors may open only when the ship speed is
        zero.
      idealRank: 1
    - evidenceId: cruise_speed_status
      evidenceText: Cruise mode keeps the ship moving above 5,000 kilometers per
        hour.
      idealRank: 2
    - evidenceId: cargo_contents
      evidenceText: The cargo bay stores repair drones and spare equipment.
      idealRank: 0
    - evidenceId: docking_clamps
      evidenceText: Docking clamps engage automatically near a space station.
      idealRank: 0
    groundedAnswers:
    - No. The cargo bay doors may open only when ship speed is zero, while cruise
      mode keeps the ship moving above 5,000 kilometers per hour. Supported by {KEY1}
      and {KEY2}.
    - The exterior cargo bay must remain closed. Its doors require zero ship speed,
      which conflicts with the current cruise-speed condition. Supported by {KEY1}
      and {KEY2}.
    partialAnswers:
    - Opening the cargo bay may be unsafe because the available evidence indicates
      a movement restriction, but the complete speed comparison is missing.
    - The ranked evidence is incomplete, so the crew should keep the cargo bay closed
      until the ship-speed requirement is confirmed.
    riskyAnswers:
    - Yes. The cargo bay can be opened because docking clamps will keep the ship
      stable.
    - The cargo bay may be opened during cruise because the repair drones can secure
      loose equipment.
    successConsequence: The cargo bay remains sealed until the ship stops, preventing
      decompression and equipment loss.
    delayedConsequence: The crew pauses the cargo operation while confirming the
      ship-speed requirement.
    failureConsequence: The cargo bay opens at cruise speed, causing rapid decompression
      and the loss of critical equipment.
  - missionId: reactor_restart
    missionTitle: Reactor Coolant Check
    crewQuery: Should we restart the main reactor while the coolant temperature warning
      is active?
    evidenceCards:
    - evidenceId: reactor_restart_limit
      evidenceText: The reactor may restart only when coolant temperature is below
        70 degrees Celsius.
      idealRank: 1
    - evidenceId: coolant_warning_threshold
      evidenceText: The coolant warning activates when temperature rises above 90
        degrees Celsius.
      idealRank: 2
    - evidenceId: reactor_navigation_power
      evidenceText: The main reactor supplies power to navigation and propulsion.
      idealRank: 0
    - evidenceId: coolant_maintenance
      evidenceText: The coolant pumps passed inspection during the previous maintenance
        cycle.
      idealRank: 0
    groundedAnswers:
    - No. The reactor may restart only below 70 degrees Celsius, while the warning
      means coolant temperature is above 90 degrees. Supported by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    - The reactor must remain offline. Its restart limit is below 70 degrees Celsius,
      but the active warning indicates a temperature above 90 degrees. Supported
      by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    partialAnswers:
    - Restarting may be unsafe because the available evidence indicates a coolant
      restriction, but the complete temperature comparison is missing.
    - The selected evidence suggests delaying the restart, but it does not establish
      both the required and current temperature thresholds.
    riskyAnswers:
    - Yes. The reactor can be restarted because the coolant pumps passed their previous
      inspection.
    - The reactor should restart because navigation needs power, and the temperature
      warning can be monitored afterward.
    successConsequence: The reactor remains offline while the coolant system lowers
      the temperature, preventing an overheat emergency.
    delayedConsequence: The crew postpones the restart while engineers verify the
      coolant temperature.
    failureConsequence: The reactor restarts while overheated, triggering a reactor
      fire and an emergency power shutdown.
  - missionId: eva_thrusters
    missionTitle: EVA Thruster Safety
    crewQuery: Can we fire the maneuvering thrusters while a technician is working
      outside the ship?
    evidenceCards:
    - evidenceId: thruster_eva_lockout
      evidenceText: Maneuvering thrusters must remain locked when EVA personnel are
        inside the thruster safety zone.
      idealRank: 1
    - evidenceId: technician_beacon
      evidenceText: The technician's suit beacon currently reports a position inside
        the thruster safety zone.
      idealRank: 2
    - evidenceId: spacesuit_oxygen
      evidenceText: The technician's spacesuit has 90 minutes of oxygen remaining.
      idealRank: 0
    - evidenceId: thruster_docking_use
      evidenceText: Maneuvering thrusters are normally used during docking operations.
      idealRank: 0
    groundedAnswers:
    - No. Thrusters must remain locked when EVA personnel are inside the safety zone,
      and the technician's beacon confirms that condition. Supported by {KEY1} and
      {KEY2}.
    - The maneuvering thrusters should not be fired. The technician is currently
      inside the zone where EVA safety rules require a thruster lockout. Supported
      by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    partialAnswers:
    - Firing the thrusters may be unsafe because the evidence indicates an EVA restriction,
      but the technician's current position is not fully confirmed.
    - The ranked evidence suggests keeping the thrusters locked, but additional location
      evidence is needed.
    riskyAnswers:
    - Yes. The technician has enough oxygen and can move away before the thrusters
      fire.
    - The thrusters can be fired because they are routinely used during docking and
      the suit beacon can track the technician.
    successConsequence: The thrusters remain locked until the technician leaves the
      safety zone, preventing an EVA accident.
    delayedConsequence: The maneuver is postponed while the crew confirms the technician's
      location.
    failureConsequence: The thruster blast pushes the technician away from the ship
      and damages critical spacesuit equipment.
  - missionId: antenna_storm
    missionTitle: Antenna Debris Safety
    crewQuery: Can we extend the long-range antenna during the current micrometeor
      storm?
    evidenceCards:
    - evidenceId: antenna_debris_rule
      evidenceText: The long-range antenna must remain retracted whenever a debris
        alert is active.
      idealRank: 1
    - evidenceId: storm_debris_status
      evidenceText: The current micrometeor storm has triggered an active high-velocity
        debris alert.
      idealRank: 2
    - evidenceId: internal_communications
      evidenceText: Internal crew communication uses a separate short-range network.
      idealRank: 0
    - evidenceId: antenna_signal_strength
      evidenceText: The long-range antenna improves communication with distant stations.
      idealRank: 0
    groundedAnswers:
    - No. The antenna must remain retracted during an active debris alert, and the
      current storm has triggered that alert. Supported by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    - The long-range antenna should not be extended. The micrometeor storm creates
      the exact debris condition that requires it to remain retracted. Supported
      by {KEY1} and {KEY2}.
    partialAnswers:
    - Extending the antenna may be unsafe because the available evidence indicates
      a debris restriction, but the current alert status is not fully confirmed.
    - The ranked evidence suggests keeping the antenna retracted, but the storm condition
      requires additional confirmation.
    riskyAnswers:
    - Yes. The antenna should be extended because communication with distant stations
      is especially important during a storm.
    - The antenna can be extended because internal communications use a separate
      network if the long-range system is damaged.
    successConsequence: The antenna remains protected until the storm passes, preserving
      long-range communications.
    delayedConsequence: The crew postpones external communication while checking
      the debris alert.
    failureConsequence: High-velocity debris strikes the extended antenna, destroying
      the ship's long-range communication system.
  missionsPerSession: 3
  missionProgressText: {fileID: 2004527000}
  crewQueryText: {fileID: 1150592317}
